1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,900
You're down to your last ad dollar

2
00:00:03,033 --> 00:00:08,333
Do you keep feeding the channel that brought the most signups last month or shift it to another one

3
00:00:08,666 --> 00:00:13,766
Most people double down but that's exactly the money most likely to go to waste

4
00:00:14,066 --> 00:00:17,833
In 2022 a company called BARK ran into this exact problem

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,633
Every month it mails dog owners a box of toys and treats

6
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:25,900
In the end it didn't let past results decide where the next dollar went

7
00:00:26,133 --> 00:00:29,000
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,133
Number 4 the equal marginal principle

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,500
Forget the theory just ask who gets the next dollar

10
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:39,133
At the time BARK had to split a limited budget across different ad channels

11
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:41,966
The easy move is to open last month's report

12
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:44,533
find the channel that brought the most signups

13
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,366
and keep feeding it the next dollar

14
00:00:47,666 --> 00:00:48,800
Sounds safe enough

15
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:51,100
after all it's already proven itself

16
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:54,433
But that report hides something easy to misread

17
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,500
It records results that already happened

18
00:00:57,666 --> 00:01:00,766
What you're deciding now is where the next dollar goes

19
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:02,833
Those are two different questions

20
00:01:02,966 --> 00:01:09,200
A channel that brought in lots of signups before won't necessarily bring in just as many new ones next

21
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:12,400
It may have already reached everyone easy to convince

22
00:01:12,533 --> 00:01:15,933
Spending more might just buy more repeat exposure

23
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:18,233
The spend on the report keeps climbing

24
00:01:18,366 --> 00:01:21,166
but the actual new signups may keep shrinking

25
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:28,033
Meanwhile another channel with less impressive numbers might still be untapped

26
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,400
Move the same dollar there and the new results could be bigger

27
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,133
BARK used Robyn Meta's open source marketing mix model

28
00:01:35,666 --> 00:01:40,200
This kind of model weighs every channel's spend and results together

29
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:43,400
The goal isn't to crown one channel as forever the best

30
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:48,766
it's to watch how each channel's response curve shifts as spending keeps rising

31
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:52,100
BARK later reported the results on Meta's case study page

32
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,600
After optimizing the budget overall signups rose 30 percent

33
00:01:56,833 --> 00:02:02,200
Put simply every 100 signups it used to get became about 130

34
00:02:02,366 --> 00:02:03,900
That number jumps out

35
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:05,733
but the fine print matters too

36
00:02:05,733 --> 00:02:08,166
It's a result the company reported itself

37
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:09,733
not a randomized controlled trial

38
00:02:09,733 --> 00:02:14,700
Meta's own page even says it can't guarantee other companies the same result

39
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:18,833
So we can't copy this case into some universal playbook

40
00:02:19,133 --> 00:02:21,700
What's actually worth keeping is the question it swapped in

41
00:02:22,133 --> 00:02:27,000
Which channel contributed most in the past no longer automatically decides where the next dollar goes

42
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:31,766
How many new signups the next dollar can still bring is the new yardstick

43
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:34,400
Total results are like a bill that's already settled

44
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:37,800
it's the next return that decides where resources flow next

45
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:40,200
This isn't just one company's marketing problem

46
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:46,733
If you've run a project managed a budget or just planned your own evening you've hit the same math

47
00:02:47,166 --> 00:02:50,566
The most important thing doesn't always deserve the next hour

48
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:54,600
The tool you use most doesn't always deserve the next upgrade

49
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:58,433
What actually wastes resources usually isn't picking a bad direction

50
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:02,233
it's a decent direction swallowing every resource that comes after

51
00:03:02,766 --> 00:03:06,966
A lot of people hear equal marginal for the first time and think it means splitting things evenly

52
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,533
Three channels so each gets a third

53
00:03:09,733 --> 00:03:12,133
Four projects so each gets one person

54
00:03:12,566 --> 00:03:15,833
Work exercise and family each get two hours

55
00:03:16,166 --> 00:03:19,233
That looks fair but it's not the equal marginal principle

56
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:22,833
In economics marginal means the extra unit on top

57
00:03:23,133 --> 00:03:24,366
One more dollar spent

58
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:25,766
One more half hour given

59
00:03:25,933 --> 00:03:27,133
One more person assigned

60
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:28,966
One more round of edits

61
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:32,000
The question isn't how much you've already put in

62
00:03:32,266 --> 00:03:35,500
it's what adding one more unit actually gets you

63
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:40,966
The equal marginal principle compares what the same extra unit returns in different places

64
00:03:41,666 --> 00:03:45,166
If A's next dollar still buys ten new orders

65
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:47,700
and B's next dollar only buys two

66
00:03:47,766 --> 00:03:49,366
then A should get it first

67
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:54,000
As A gets more budget the easiest people to reach start running out

68
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:59,300
The next dollar's orders might drop from ten to eight then down to five

69
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:05,000
Once B's next dollar also brings in five the payoff from shifting more money shrinks

70
00:04:05,166 --> 00:04:07,333
That's the equal in equal marginal

71
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,533
Not that each side gets the same amount of money

72
00:04:10,566 --> 00:04:15,533
but that the last unit of resource returns roughly the same wherever it lands

73
00:04:15,566 --> 00:04:20,233
OpenStax's economics textbook makes the same point with 56 dollars

74
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:24,566
A guy named José has to spend it all on t shirts and movies

75
00:04:24,833 --> 00:04:26,600
A t shirt costs 14 dollars

76
00:04:26,766 --> 00:04:28,400
A movie costs 7 dollars

77
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:32,700
If he spends all 56 dollars on t shirts he can buy four

78
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:37,000
But the fourth shirt brings a lot less satisfaction than the first

79
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,766
The textbook uses sample numbers to show that shift

80
00:04:39,966 --> 00:04:43,800
Skipping the fourth shirt costs him 18 units of satisfaction

81
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:48,233
The 14 dollars saved buys the first two movies

82
00:04:48,533 --> 00:04:51,966
Those two movies together bring 31 units of satisfaction

83
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:54,633
Lose 18 on one side gain 31 on the other

84
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,766
Make that swap and total satisfaction actually goes up by 13

85
00:04:57,833 --> 00:05:00,800
These units aren't some real world happiness meter

86
00:05:00,966 --> 00:05:03,900
they just lay the comparison out where you can see it

87
00:05:04,066 --> 00:05:06,800
Don't ask whether t shirts or movies are the nobler choice

88
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:11,333
ask which side gains more from the next dollar you put in

89
00:05:11,433 --> 00:05:14,566
The textbook eventually turns this rule into a formula

90
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:21,533
Once every dollar spent returns roughly equal marginal utility across goods the budget stops improving by rearranging

91
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:23,366
You can forget the formula

92
00:05:23,366 --> 00:05:25,133
but remember the move behind it

93
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:29,166
Take an equal sized unit of resource and compare its next move across every option

94
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:35,566
If you only look at which line item spent the most when the bill arrives you'll likely cut the wrong one

95
00:05:35,966 --> 00:05:40,233
A big total might just mean it's solving problems every single day

96
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:45,400
while another line item costs almost nothing yet adds zero new value

97
00:05:45,633 --> 00:05:46,966
The reverse holds too

98
00:05:47,033 --> 00:05:52,133
A project that contributed a lot before doesn't mean its next hour today is still the most valuable

99
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:55,000
The equal marginal principle doesn't decide what matters for you

100
00:05:55,066 --> 00:05:58,600
it just stops past results from posing as future returns

101
00:05:59,366 --> 00:06:02,133
The hard part isn't understanding the rule

102
00:06:02,166 --> 00:06:06,966
it's actually moving resources away from the best performer at the meeting table

103
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:10,600
The place already delivering results is usually the loudest one

104
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:11,666
It has the reports

105
00:06:11,666 --> 00:06:12,700
it has an owner

106
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:15,233
it has a growth curve you can point to

107
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:17,933
Keep funding it and at least you can explain the decision

108
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,933
The new direction that hasn't started yet has no track record to speak for it

109
00:06:22,066 --> 00:06:26,000
and if it fails the blame lands squarely on whoever made the call

110
00:06:26,166 --> 00:06:28,833
So a lot of allocation plans look careful on paper

111
00:06:28,966 --> 00:06:31,566
but really they're just rewarding a visible past

112
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:34,533
You can see the same tension in research funding

113
00:06:34,633 --> 00:06:40,500
In 2016 the US National Institute of General Medical Sciences updated an analysis of its grants

114
00:06:40,666 --> 00:06:48,166
It looked at the NIH funding a group of researchers received and the papers and impact metrics that followed

115
00:06:48,433 --> 00:06:54,733
In this sample the top 5 percent of funded researchers received 25.2 percent of the money

116
00:06:54,833 --> 00:07:01,600
In other words for every 20 researchers the single top earner took home about a quarter of the funding

117
00:07:01,733 --> 00:07:05,800
The top 20 percent combined took home half the funding

118
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,933
Concentration by itself isn't wrong

119
00:07:07,933 --> 00:07:10,266
Some experiments need expensive equipment

120
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:14,333
some involve human or animal subjects where costs are just higher

121
00:07:14,533 --> 00:07:20,166
What actually matters is whether output keeps pace as the money keeps growing

122
00:07:20,933 --> 00:07:25,233
The analysis found total paper counts do rise with funding overall

123
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:29,433
but at higher funding levels the growth doesn't keep up with the funding growth

124
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:34,133
Papers per million dollars actually drops as funding rises

125
00:07:34,233 --> 00:07:36,766
More money may still mean more output

126
00:07:36,766 --> 00:07:41,000
just that each new dollar buys less extra output than the one before it

127
00:07:41,233 --> 00:07:45,566
The researchers also compared citations per paper and relative citation impact

128
00:07:45,666 --> 00:07:50,200
Those numbers didn't scale up proportionally with funding either

129
00:07:50,433 --> 00:07:53,800
Papers and citations obviously can't capture all the value of research

130
00:07:54,066 --> 00:07:57,966
and group level data can't tell you whether one specific lab deserves funding

131
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:01,700
So this research isn't a knife for cutting big projects

132
00:08:01,766 --> 00:08:07,666
it's a reminder that the more concentrated resources get the more you need to recheck what the next dollar actually returns

133
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:10,766
The institute floated a possible direction based on this

134
00:08:10,966 --> 00:08:14,233
Instead of keeping large sums concentrated on a small group

135
00:08:14,366 --> 00:08:19,133
backing more researchers at moderate funding levels might be the better investment

136
00:08:19,133 --> 00:08:24,233
This conclusion feels uncomfortable because it runs into a very familiar habit

137
00:08:24,433 --> 00:08:27,600
We like giving resources to people who've already proven themselves

138
00:08:27,833 --> 00:08:30,933
Companies keep piling headcount onto the star project

139
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,166
teams keep saving meeting time for whatever's getting the most attention

140
00:08:34,566 --> 00:08:39,433
and you might spend tonight's last hour on the proposal you've already revised three times

141
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:42,100
Not because that's where the hour is needed most

142
00:08:42,166 --> 00:08:46,200
but because the file's already open and the edits show up right away

143
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,200
If you've had nights like that don't chalk it up to weak self control

144
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:53,233
you just got pulled in by the clearest feedback in the room

145
00:08:53,533 --> 00:09:00,233
But going from 85 to 86 and getting a zero started aren't the same kind of change

146
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:03,200
The loudest spot is always first in line for the next unit

147
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:06,633
the quietest gap might be where that unit actually matters

148
00:09:06,866 --> 00:09:10,633
This isn't a rule against strong performers or key projects either

149
00:09:10,866 --> 00:09:17,533
If the channel that brought the most signups still returns the most on its next dollar it should keep getting funded

150
00:09:17,833 --> 00:09:20,166
The equal marginal principle doesn't decide winners in advance

151
00:09:20,333 --> 00:09:23,466
it just asks that every time you add more you compare the next unit again

152
00:09:23,466 --> 00:09:27,366
In daily life the equal marginal principle doesn't need a complicated formula

153
00:09:27,533 --> 00:09:30,300
Just pause for ten seconds before you add more

154
00:09:30,466 --> 00:09:33,366
Shrink the resource down to one unit you can actually see

155
00:09:33,633 --> 00:09:35,433
Don't write I'll try a little harder

156
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:37,700
Write give this 30 more minutes

157
00:09:37,833 --> 00:09:39,633
Don't write I'll put in a bit more money

158
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:41,233
Write put in 100 more dollars

159
00:09:41,533 --> 00:09:43,566
Don't write the team needs more support

160
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:46,300
Write give this project half a day of one person's time

161
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:48,333
Then write two lines in your notes

162
00:09:48,333 --> 00:09:52,033
What exactly changes if this unit goes to the choice in front of you

163
00:09:52,033 --> 00:09:55,666
and what exactly changes if the same unit goes to the other gap instead

164
00:09:55,666 --> 00:09:58,033
Say a proposal has already been revised three times

165
00:09:58,066 --> 00:10:01,833
Another 30 minutes on it might just make two pages look neater

166
00:10:02,166 --> 00:10:08,166
The same 30 minutes spent prepping tomorrow's hardest task could get something unstarted off the ground

167
00:10:08,166 --> 00:10:11,566
You don't need to rank which task is more noble here

168
00:10:11,633 --> 00:10:14,633
you're just comparing what those 30 minutes change

169
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,100
If another half hour on it can genuinely prevent a major mistake tomorrow

170
00:10:19,233 --> 00:10:21,433
then give the time to the proposal

171
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:25,133
If it'll only make an already clear page prettier

172
00:10:25,133 --> 00:10:28,233
while the other option can cut a real risk

173
00:10:28,366 --> 00:10:30,533
then move that half hour over there

174
00:10:30,666 --> 00:10:32,833
You can judge spending the same way

175
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:39,366
Before upgrading your everyday software to the top tier write down one feature you'd actually use more

176
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:43,333
then write down what problem that same money could solve somewhere else

177
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:47,933
If the first one can't name a concrete change while the second one changes the outcome right away

178
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,933
the answer's usually already clear

179
00:10:50,066 --> 00:10:51,933
The same goes for managing a team

180
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,833
Don't automatically drop the new hire onto whatever project gets the most attention

181
00:10:57,033 --> 00:11:02,433
First check whether it's actually stuck on headcount or on equipment access or decisions

182
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,900
If the bottleneck isn't people adding one more just adds another round of coordination

183
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,733
This has its limits too

184
00:11:09,233 --> 00:11:15,233
Safety compliance and promises you've already made can't be cut just based on short term payoff

185
00:11:15,366 --> 00:11:19,400
Long term learning and infrastructure may also show little change in the short run

186
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:22,600
So we're not comparing which feels better in the next minute

187
00:11:22,633 --> 00:11:27,766
but what this resource is most likely to change over the right time frame

188
00:11:27,766 --> 00:11:30,000
You don't need the perfect answer on the first try

189
00:11:30,066 --> 00:11:32,600
real world returns don't all show up on a report anyway

190
00:11:32,733 --> 00:11:35,166
When you don't have enough information move a small piece first

191
00:11:35,266 --> 00:11:36,400
Try it for a night

192
00:11:36,466 --> 00:11:37,600
try it with 100 dollars

193
00:11:37,666 --> 00:11:39,166
try one staffing change

194
00:11:39,233 --> 00:11:41,700
then see if the outcome actually shifts

195
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:43,966
Good allocation isn't about being right every time

196
00:11:44,033 --> 00:11:49,533
it's letting resources flow from where returns have thinned out to where one key unit is still missing

197
00:11:49,533 --> 00:11:51,800
Now back to BARK's budget meeting

198
00:11:51,833 --> 00:11:57,333
That ad money from the start would you still hand it straight to last month's top channel

199
00:11:57,666 --> 00:12:01,033
There's still a channel on screen that brought the most signups in the past

200
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:02,900
Its track record hasn't disappeared

201
00:12:03,033 --> 00:12:06,966
it just doesn't automatically claim the next dollar anymore

202
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:13,433
Next time you're about to add a little more money time or people pause for ten seconds first

203
00:12:13,666 --> 00:12:15,900
Don't ask which choice was best in the past

204
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,733
ask where this next unit would actually make a difference

205
00:12:19,966 --> 00:12:23,200
Next time we'll look at economic principle number five

206
00:12:23,466 --> 00:12:29,333
Why does the first taste of something you love feel so sweet while the third often just feels like habit
